From f00f2ecba891b8a0a9d2cc2e4272193df57bd23a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emjupy <dev@example.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 18:33:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add emjupy-split-cell on C-c s, and fix cell lookup at a
 boundary

Splitting divides the source at point: text before stays, text from
point on moves to a new cell of the same type below, and point follows
it there. Output stays with the top half along with its execution count
-- neither half has been run as it now stands, but discarding results
the user did not ask to discard is worse, and C-c l clears them.

Writing it surfaced a real bug underneath. Commands found the cell at
point through the emjupy-cell text property, which is stamped once at
render time; plain insert does not carry it onto new text, so anything
typed at a cell boundary -- the end of its last line, the obvious place
to type -- was invisible to the lookup. Split refused outright with
'Point is not in a cell', and insert-cell-below failed SILENTLY: finding
no current cell, it appended to the end of the notebook instead of
inserting below.

emjupy--cell-at-point now finds the cell from the overlays, which move
with insertions by construction, falling back to the property. Every
lookup site in emjupy-cells.el and emjupy-kernel.el uses it, so execute,
delete, insert, move and cycle-type all stop losing track of freshly
typed text.
